    "q-138609": {
      "dom_id": "step-q138609",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}11 & 13 & 15 \\\\ 10 & 7 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & ⋯ & {x}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis given by \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${b}_{i}={x}_{i}-m$ for $i=1,2,…,n,$ and $m$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}39 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
