      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 13 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}30 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}18 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
