    "q-138640": {
      "dom_id": "step-q138640",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 8 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
