    "q-137298": {
      "dom_id": "step-q137298",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}74 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
